Z||000000||Denise||05-26-2000||08:20 AM||||the 3800 is a commonly availble engine right?  And it does bolt up to our transmissions? I can't ever afford the SC.  But! can the SC be added later? or are the componants set up just for that partical engine?   As always, the hookup of the harness is the big stumbling block right?  ||||reg||9
Z||000001||batboy||05-26-2000||08:42 AM||||Denise, the 3800 V6 is an excellent engine known for it's reliability and torque. This engine is commonly used in most late model GM full size cars (Bonneville, Grand Prix, Delta 98, Park Avenue, etc.) The SC version is not quite as common and a bit more expensive. I believe the engine does physically bolt up to the tranny. Like you mentioned though, wiring harness is a pain and motor mounts need changed and a host of other little things to make everything fit right. If you do buy an engine, get a totally complete motor with all the engine wiring harness and ECM. I'm going to list a few links about this swap. I've heard good and bad things about the ACE conversion kits, but their site has some good info.<P> <A HREF="http://www.americancustomeng.com/3800-V6.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.americancustomeng.com/3800-V6.htm</A> <P> <A HREF="http://members.home.net/gene-down/project.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.home.net/gene-down/project.htm</A> <P> <A HREF="http://www.terranexus.com/fieroman/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.terranexus.com/fieroman/</A> <P> <A HREF="http://members.xoom.com/fierov6/OSG/mod.3800-V6.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.xoom.com/fierov6/OSG/mod.3800-V6.html</A> <P> <A HREF="http://www.netvista.net/~jlatteri/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.netvista.net/~jlatteri/index.html</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||MikeE||06-01-2000||08:31 AM||||Denise,<BR>The 3800 is a good swap because the engine is reliable, very torquey, and smooth because balance shafts were added in 88 which noticably helped.  The series II had 205HP so it's only 35HP down from the late model SC and actually the same as the early SC.  However, all series II engines have the OBD II system which is more difficult to modify.  The pre-94 3800 and 3800 SC would be easier to modify if that is planned but in stock form they are down on power compared to the later models.  I have been considering various swaps, incuding the 3800 and the 3.4 DOHC and have been assured by many people, some of them GM mechanics, that the Muncie 5sp from a V6 (commonly called a Getrag) has the same bolt pattern as the 3800 so it will bolt up but I'm not sure if the input shaft length is correct.  I do know you will need a custom flywheel since the 3800 never came with a manual trans and a stronger clutch and pressure plate would be advisable.  A kit would make it much easier since they would presumably have addressed all these concerns and should also have a custom wiring harness.  A company called Pisa makes a swap kit.  I have a catalog lying around here somewhere so as soon as I find it I will email more info.  If you don't mind my adding my two cents, I personally think it would be far easier and more reliable to swap in the 4sp auto w/OD from the 3800 but I understand the desire to have a manual.  I'm not really in love with shifting (a year of cross country tractor-trailer driving cured that), but I do like the greater control a manual affords and I especially like the more direct feel when accelerating.  Although an auto can be made to feel very close to that it will no longer shift or engage smoothly but rather with an abrupt jerk.  At least with a manual you can control that with an educated left foot.<P>Aloha, Mike||||reg||1
Z||000003||Dan86gt3.8||06-02-2000||02:59 AM||||Go with the 3.8, I did I love it.  Sometimes I wish I had the manual trans, but when I am cruising with one arm out the window and the other on the wheel and all i have to do is mash the gas its great.  But I think it would be faster with the manual trans.  And yes they bolt right up, no problems except for the flywheel.  Anyways take care and for god sakes just do the swap <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
